  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1247 AM CDT Thu Oct 15 2026\n\nFog will be the first order of business this morning. There is a\nshallow stratus layer right along the Ohio River that seems to\nbe preventing parts of the area from really dropping, but south\nof that where skies are clearer and rain fell today visibilities\nare dropping quickly. 00z Nashville sounding and the available\nmodel soundings show moisture increasing with height through the\nlowest few hundred feet of the column. Winds at the surface are\nnear calm through the next few hours. Issued a Dense Fog \nAdvisory for parts of the area about an hour ago and will be \nwatching other areas to see if the stratus clears out enough to \nget dense fog at the surface.\n\nA surface low forming over Oklahoma tonight generates warm\nfrontogenetic forcing over the area starting around dawn which\nmay lead to scattered showers. The best chance appears to be\nover SEMO and southern Illinois in the morning with this\nparticular activity. By late afternoon the main surface cold\nfront starts to move quickly towards the area - increasing \nchances for showers and isolated thunderstorms from the \nnorthwest. Instability is modest overall with this frontal \npassage but PWAT values remain up above 1.8 inches so efficient \nrainfall rates appear likely with any showers that do get going.\nRight along the warm front and close to the surface low passing\nto the northwest there appears to be a small window for \nsurface-based convection. The most favorable zone for this may \nbe just north and northwest of the area, but there is a marginal\nrisk for wind/hail or isolated tornado activity based on the \nprojected shear/instability/hodograph shape.\n\nAfter that conditions get pretty pumpkin-spiced with highs \nlikely struggling to reach above 60 Friday and Saturday amid a \nnorthwest breeze. The current forecast calls for highs today to \nbe about 25 to 30 degrees warmer than projected highs on Friday.\nOvernight lows Friday and especially Saturday night appear \nheaded for the 30s. Conditions Saturday night appear cold enough\nfor frost to be a factor.\n\nSoutherly flow returns by Sunday/Monday and conditions start to\nmoderate with dry and relatively pleasant conditions expected\nthrough the early part of the week.",
